  "query_preview": "..\n\nWeak flow aloft associated with a transient rex configuration passes \nover the Great Lakes today, with ensemble MSLP output indicating a \n+2 sigma ridge centering over southern Ontario and Quebec. Low-level \neasterly wind holds thermal troughing over SE MI with 850mb temp \nstagnant in the single digits Celsius. A weak cutoff low passing \nthrough the Ohio Valley is causing moisture to retrograde east to \nwest across the southern forecast area, bringing mostly cloudy skies \nat least through the morning hours before drier air starts to work \nback in. Scattered light showers remain possible mainly south of I-\n94 where mid-level deformation coincides with low-level convergence \ndownwind of Lake Erie. TDTW radar data shows these shallow showers \nmoving into the Luna Pier vicinity already. Given the cloud cover, \nhighs in the mid 60s are expected for much of the area. Farther \nnorth toward the Saginaw Valley and interior Thumb, mostly sunny \nskies should prevail today and boost temps slightly toward 70 \ndegrees.\n\nLongwave ridge cresting over eastern Canada allows the resident \nsurface high to hold in place through the end of the week. \nPersistent wind direction from the east maintains a cooler pattern, \nthough a warming trend is likely as increasing insolation provides \nsome modification to the air mass each day. The surface high is \nforecast to expand more into the Great Lakes by Friday and Saturday \nas greater subsidence spreads overhead in response to a coastal low \ntracking up the eastern seaboard. This allows each day to become \nless breezy as the gradient subsides. Dew points holding in the \n40s/50s and mostly clear skies will favor crisp nights with lows in \nthe 40s outside of the urban heat island. Split flow aloft looks to \ndominate the late weekend and early week, favoring continued dry and \nbenign conditions as high pressure remains parked overhead.",
